<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Smith (explorer)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1580">1580</a>–<a href="/years/1631">1631</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Captain John Smith</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Smith was an English soldier and explorer who helped found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> in 1607 and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> colony as president of its council in 1608 and 1609. His leadership kept the struggling settlement alive through its early hardships. His writings, including the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pocahontas">Pocahontas</a>, and his maps of Virginia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> shaped how the colonies were seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-smith-explorer.jpg" alt="John Smith (explorer)" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Book by Capt. John Smith, engraver uncertain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houghton_STC_22790_-_Generall_Historie_of_Virginia,_New_England,_and_the_Summer_Isles,_John_Smith.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
